---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import EndOfLifeChecker from '@/components/tools/reference/EndOfLifeChecker.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="end-of-life-checker">
  <EndOfLifeChecker client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the end-of-life checker</h2>
    <p>
      Pick a language, framework, database, operating system or application, and type the version
      you run. The end-of-life checker tells you whether that version still gets fixes and until when.
      The timeline shows the recent release cycles: black while a version has full support, grey while
      it gets security fixes only, and the line marks today.
    </p>
    <h2>Support phases</h2>
    <p>
      Most projects support each release in two phases. During active support a release gets bug fixes
      and small improvements. During security support it gets fixes for vulnerabilities only. After the
      end-of-life date there are no fixes at all, and a newly found vulnerability stays open. Some
      projects have one phase only, and some publish no dates.
    </p>
    <h2>Long-term support releases</h2>
    <p>
      An LTS release has a longer support window than the releases around it. Node.js even-numbered
      releases, Ubuntu .04 releases of even years and Java LTS versions are examples. For servers, pick an
      LTS release and plan the upgrade before its end date.
    </p>
    <h2>Where the dates come from</h2>
    <p>
      The data is from endoflife.date, a community project that records the published support policy of
      each product. The site downloads it every week. Commercial extended support, for example paid
      security updates for an old Windows or RHEL release, is not included.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
